title: UNC6040 / Salesforce Vishing
page: breaches/unc6040-salesforce-vishing.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Google Threat Intelligence first reported UNC6040 in June 2025: callers posing as IT support talked employees into authorizing an attacker-controlled Data Loader, with no Salesforce vulnerability exploited.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: None | Salesforce vulnerabilities exploited | at any point in the chain
stat: Google | was among the victims | its own corporate Salesforce instance
stat: 72h | bitcoin deadline in the extortion | which often came months after the theft
Google Threat Intelligence first reported UNC6040 in June 2025, as a financially motivated cluster.
notes:
Start with the points the page leads with.

First, no Salesforce vulnerability was exploited at any point. Employees were talked into authorizing the attacker's app instead, and the rest of the deck shows how.

Second, the victims included Google's own corporate Salesforce instance, along with fashion houses and technology firms. The page names no other victim.

Third, the extortion that followed set a bitcoin deadline of 72 hours. Months often separated the theft from the extortion; the page's summary says it arrived separately, and sometimes months later. Google tracks that follow-on activity as a separate cluster, UNC6240.

Google Threat Intelligence first reported UNC6040 in June 2025. The page describes it as a financially motivated cluster that compromised Salesforce tenants. A tenant is one customer's own instance of a shared platform, here Salesforce. Vishing, the word in the deck's title, is voice phishing: phishing by telephone.

== What happened
layout: text
Operators phoned employees at English-speaking branches of multinationals, posing as IT support, and walked them through Salesforce's own connected-app authorization page. A connection code they entered authorized an attacker-controlled Data Loader build, which then exported data with their permissions.
notes:
Here is the page's summary in short.

Operators phoned employees at English-speaking branches of multinationals, impersonating IT support. They walked them through Salesforce's own connected-app authorization page and had them enter a connection code. That code authorized an attacker-controlled build of Salesforce's Data Loader tool. From that point the app queried and exported data with the victim's own permissions.

Data Loader is Salesforce's legitimate bulk import and export tool. The page says it connects through OAuth as a connected app, and a later slide explains OAuth in general terms.

Victims included Google's own corporate Salesforce instance, along with fashion houses and technology firms. Extortion arrived separately, and sometimes months later.

The page gives one date: Google Threat Intelligence first reported UNC6040 in June 2025. It gives no dates for individual intrusions, so this deck has no timeline. The page closes its summary with the point the deck returns to: the chain that matters here is the one where a legitimate consent screen, shown to a real employee who was told to expect it, is the entire attack.

== Two clusters, as Google tracks them
layout: compare
col: UNC6040 | A financially motivated cluster, first reported in June 2025 | Phoned employees, had them authorize the app, took the data | Placed calls over Mullvad VPN addresses and TOR
col: UNC6240 | The follow-on extortion, often months after the theft | Bitcoin within 72 hours, by call or email to employees | Claims ShinyHunters affiliation; Google treats it as likely pressure tactics
Google tracks the extortion as a separate cluster, which suggests UNC6040 sells or hands off access rather than extorting directly.
notes:
Two names appear for two kinds of activity, and both come from Google's tracking.

UNC6040 is the cluster Google Threat Intelligence first reported in June 2025: the vishing calls, the connected-app authorization and the data theft. Its calls were placed over Mullvad VPN addresses and TOR.

UNC6240 is the name Google uses for the follow-on extortion. It demanded bitcoin within 72 hours, by call or email to employees, often months after the theft. Google tracks it as a separate cluster, which suggests UNC6040 sells or hands off access rather than extorting directly. Keep that word, suggests: the page does not say which.

UNC6240 also claims affiliation with ShinyHunters. Google treats that claim as likely pressure tactics rather than established fact, so do not present ShinyHunters as the attacker. The page draws a lesson from it at stage 06.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | A fake IT support call | T1566.004
step: 02 | Execution | A genuine consent screen | T1204.001
step: 03 | Persistence | A grant that outlives the call | T1550.001
step: 04 | Exfiltration | Small chunks, then whole tables | T1213
step: 05 | Lateral Movement | Okta and Microsoft 365 | T1078.004
step: 06 | Impact | Delayed extortion | T1657
Stage 05 applies where the operator also took credentials on the call.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's six stages, each with its phase and an ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is the phone call from someone posing as IT support. Stage 02 is the genuine Salesforce consent screen, where the victim authorized the attacker's app. Stage 03 is the grant that keeps working after the call ends. Stage 04 is the export, in small chunks first and then whole tables. Stage 05 is the move into Okta and Microsoft 365. Stage 06 is the extortion, often months later.

Stage 05 depends on a detail from stage 01. In some intrusions the operator also asked for credentials and MFA codes during the call, and the page says credentials were reused where the operator had harvested them.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order. The page does not trace one victim through every stage, and it gives no times. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Callers posing as IT support phoned employees
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.004 | Phishing: Spearphishing Voice
technique: T1684.001 | Social Engineering: Impersonation
fact: Pretext | internal IT support, by telephone
fact: Targets | English-speaking branches of multinational corporations
fact: Infrastructure | Mullvad VPN addresses and TOR
fact: Sometimes also | an Okta phishing panel for credentials and MFA codes
Voice, the page says, carries pressure email cannot, defeats mail-based controls and leaves no artifact to inspect afterwards.
notes:
Stage 01 is the phone call. The page maps it to T1566.004, Phishing: Spearphishing Voice, and T1684.001, Social Engineering: Impersonation.

Operators called employees at English-speaking branches of multinational corporations, impersonating internal IT support. The page explains the targeting: in those branches an unfamiliar accent or an unknown internal name is unremarkable, and staff routinely take instruction from a central IT function they have never met.

The page says voice is chosen deliberately. It carries urgency and social pressure that email cannot, it defeats every mail-based control an organization has bought, and it leaves no artifact for a security team to inspect afterwards.

Calls were placed over Mullvad VPN addresses and TOR. Mullvad is a commercial VPN service, and TOR is an anonymity network. In some intrusions the operator also stood up an Okta phishing panel and simply asked for credentials and MFA codes during the call. Okta is an identity provider, and MFA is multi-factor authentication. Those credentials come back at stage 05.

The page calls this the same opening as Scattered Spider / MGM two years earlier, aimed at a different endpoint.

== What ATT&CK calls spearphishing voice
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1566.004 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1566/004/
credit: Also from ATT&CK T1684.001, Social Engineering: Impersonation (attack.mitre.org)
ATT&CK describes spearphishing voice as manipulating a user into providing access to systems through a phone call or other voice communications. It frequently involves posing as a trusted source, or creating a sense of urgency or alarm.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK says adversaries may use voice communications to ultimately gain access to victim systems. Spearphishing voice is a specific variant of spearphishing. What sets it apart is manipulating a user into providing access to systems through a phone call or other forms of voice communication.

Spearphishing frequently involves social engineering techniques, ATT&CK says, such as posing as a trusted source, or creating a sense of urgency or alarm for the recipient.

The trusted-source part has its own entry, Impersonation. ATT&CK says adversaries may impersonate a trusted person or organization to persuade and trick a target into performing some action on their behalf. Its examples include impersonating a known sender such as an executive, a colleague or a third-party vendor.

The page maps its stage 01 to both techniques.

Also from ATT&CK T1684.001: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1684/001/

== Victims authorized a modified Data Loader
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Execution
technique: T1204.001 | User Execution: Malicious Link
technique: T1528 | Steal Application Access Token
fact: Tool impersonated | Salesforce Data Loader, for bulk import and export
fact: Mechanism | a connected app, authorized by a supplied connection code
fact: The build | modified, and not published by Salesforce
fact: Later | custom Python scripts doing the same job
No product vulnerability was needed: the victim authorized the app through Salesforce's own flow.
notes:
Stage 02 is the consent. The page maps it to T1204.001, User Execution: Malicious Link, and T1528, Steal Application Access Token.

Salesforce's Data Loader is a legitimate bulk import and export tool that connects through OAuth as a connected app. A connected app is an outside application linked to a Salesforce tenant. The operator supplied a modified build with altered name and branding, not published by Salesforce.

The caller directed the victim to Salesforce's genuine connected-app setup page and had them enter an attacker-supplied connection code. That authorized the modified Data Loader. The page calls this the reason no product vulnerability was needed.

Later, the group moved from Data Loader to custom Python scripts doing the same job.

The next slide sets out what the employee saw, in the page's words, and the slide after it explains OAuth access tokens in general terms.

== Everything the employee saw was real
layout: compare
col: Genuine, per the page | The Salesforce domain | The consent page and the authorization flow | The tool's apparent name
col: The only false element | Who was asking, and why
The page says consent screens are a poor control at the exact moment they matter most.
notes:
This slide uses the page's own split.

Everything the employee saw was real: the genuine Salesforce domain, the genuine consent page, a tool name they recognized, and a helpful colleague on the phone explaining why it was needed. The only false element was the identity of the person asking, and why.

The page draws its point from this. Consent screens are a poor control at the exact moment they matter most, because a user who has been told to expect one and given a plausible reason will approve it. The interface offers them nothing that distinguishes this app from the real thing.

The page's first defense takes the decision away from users: a user who cannot grant the consent cannot be talked into granting it. It comes back in the first discussion question.

== What an OAuth access token lets an application do
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1528 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1528/
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entry for OAuth 2.0 (csoh.org)
OAuth 2.0 is a delegated authorization framework that issues access tokens. ATT&CK says an OAuth access token lets a third-party application work with a user's data, in the ways the application requested, without the application obtaining the user's credentials.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK and the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The csoh.org glossary defines OAuth 2.0 as a delegated authorization framework that issues access tokens, and notes that the specification does not define authentication.

ATT&CK says application access tokens are used to make authorized API requests on behalf of a user or service, and are commonly used to access resources in cloud and software-as-a-service applications. API stands for application programming interface.

An OAuth access token, ATT&CK says, enables a third-party application to interact with resources containing user data, in the ways the application requested, without the application obtaining the user's credentials.

ATT&CK adds that token theft can occur through social engineering, in which case user action may be required to grant access, and that adversaries can construct a malicious application designed to be granted access with a target user's OAuth token.

The page maps its stage 02 to this technique.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-oauth-2-0

== The grant outlives the call, logout and password changes
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Persistence
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
fact: What persists | an OAuth grant with its own credential, tied to the app
fact: Not revoked by | logout, password change, or later MFA challenges
fact: Visible to the user | effectively nothing after the initial approval
fact: Real kill switch | only revoking the app, which requires knowing it exists
The call ends and the access does not.
notes:
Stage 03 is what the consent left behind. The page maps it to T1550.001, Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token.

Once consent was granted, the app made API calls as that user, independent of the session, the password, and any subsequent MFA prompt. The page explains why. An OAuth grant is a durable authorization that operates on its own credential, so it survives the user logging out, changing their password, or completing an MFA challenge for something else.

Nothing in the victim's normal experience signals the grant's existence, and nothing in the standard account-hygiene playbook revokes it. The only reliable kill switch, the page says, is revoking the connected app itself, and that requires knowing it is there.

The page compares this with Salesloft Drift and Vercel / Context.ai, two other kill chains on csoh.org. The same structural property made those effective, it says. Here it was reached by talking a person into creating the grant, rather than by stealing one that already existed.

== Google saw small chunks, then whole tables
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1213 | Data from Information Repositories
technique: T1030 | Data Transfer Size Limits
fact: Phase 1 | small chunks, to learn which objects mattered and the schema
fact: Phase 2 | sharply increased volume, to take entire tables
fact: Proficiency | varied noticeably between intrusions
fact: One victim | lost only about 10% of the data before detection cut it short
The page says detection latency directly determined loss, and volumetric alerting on exports worked where it existed.
notes:
Stage 04 is the export. The page maps it to T1213, Data from Information Repositories, and T1030, Data Transfer Size Limits.

Google observed a deliberate two-phase pattern. First came small chunk sizes, for reconnaissance: working out which objects mattered and what the schema looked like. Then came a sharp increase in volume, to take entire tables once the operator knew what was worth having. A schema is the structure of the data: its objects and their fields.

Proficiency varied noticeably between intrusions, which the page says suggests different hands on different jobs. In one case only about ten percent of the data was retrieved before detection cut it short.

The page calls that variance the encouraging detail, because it means detection latency directly determined loss. The control that mattered was volumetric alerting on export activity, and where it existed it worked. The page names the control: Transaction Security Policies alerting on large exports. They come back with the defenses.

== Salesforce was not the destination
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Source | credentials and MFA codes captured during the vishing call
fact: Reused against | Okta and Microsoft 365 accounts
fact: Where | the operator had also harvested credentials during the call
fact: Why Okta matters | the identity provider governs everything federated behind it
A vishing call that yielded credentials puts the identity estate in scope, the page says, not just the application the caller named.
notes:
Stage 05 is where the intrusion left Salesforce. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts.

Where the operator had also harvested end-user credentials during the call, those were reused to move into other cloud platforms, Okta and Microsoft 365 in particular.

The page says that turns a CRM data-theft incident into an identity incident, and that it is the reason scoping this to "we lost some Salesforce records" was usually wrong. CRM is customer relationship management, the kind of system Salesforce is.

Okta is the higher-value target of the two by a distance, the page says, because control of the identity provider is control of everything federated behind it. The next slide explains identity providers and federation in general terms.

The page's rule for responders: any response to a vishing call that yielded credentials has to assume the identity estate is in scope, not just the application the caller named.

== What an identity provider and federation are
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, IdP | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-idp
An identity provider, or IdP, is the system that authenticates users. Federation is trusting tokens from a foreign IdP. Single sign-on means signing in once and getting into many applications without re-entering credentials.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines an identity provider, or IdP, as the system that authenticates users. Okta is one of the examples it gives.

It defines federation as trusting tokens from a foreign IdP.

Single sign-on, or SSO, is signing in once and getting into many applications without re-entering credentials.

Put together, these explain the page's phrase "everything federated behind it": the applications that trust an identity provider's tokens.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-federation and https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-sso

== Extortion often came months later
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Impact
technique: T1657 | Financial Theft
fact: Cluster | UNC6240, which Google tracks separately from UNC6040
fact: Demand | bitcoin within 72 hours, by call or email to employees
fact: Branding | claimed ShinyHunters ties, likely for pressure, per Google
fact: Escalation | the actors were preparing a data leak site
A familiar crew name in a ransom note is a marketing claim; treating it as attribution sends an investigation the wrong way.
notes:
Stage 06 is the extortion. The page maps it to T1657, Financial Theft.

The gap between theft and extortion frequently ran to months. Google tracks the monetization as a separate cluster, UNC6240, which suggests UNC6040 sells or hands off access rather than extorting directly.

Demands came by call or email to employees and wanted bitcoin within 72 hours. They were sent from addresses including shinycorp@tuta[.]com and shinygroup@tuta[.]com, and asserted affiliation with ShinyHunters, a claim Google treats as likely pressure tactics rather than established fact. The actors were also preparing a data leak site for additional pressure.

The page draws two practical consequences. First, an organization can be months past a successful intrusion with no signal at all, so a vishing report from a single employee deserves a real investigation rather than a note. Second, a familiar crew name in a ransom note is a marketing claim, and treating it as attribution will send an investigation in the wrong direction.

== The chain that matters: a genuine consent screen
layout: text
The page says the chain that matters is one where a legitimate consent screen, shown to a real employee told to expect it, is the entire attack. No Salesforce vulnerability was exploited at any point.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws in its summary.

No Salesforce vulnerability was exploited at any point. The employee saw a genuine domain and a genuine consent page, and a caller told them to expect it. The only false element was who was asking.

What the consent created then outlasted the conversation. The page says such a grant survives logout, a password change and later MFA challenges, and that the only reliable kill switch is revoking the connected app.

The page says the same structural property made Salesloft Drift and Vercel / Context.ai so effective. In this chain, a person was talked into creating the grant rather than having an existing one stolen.

The defenses that follow start with who is allowed to grant consent at all, and end with an audit of grants nobody approved.

== Six defenses against six stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06
row: Allowlist connected apps, restrict who authorizes | 02
row: API Enabled treated as export | 02 | 04
row: Alerts on export volume | 04
row: Access bound to network location | 02
row: The human process hardened | 01 | 02
row: Identity estate scoped, grants audited | 03 | 05
No defense's instruction names stage 06's extortion. The human-process defense cites its delay as a reason to make reporting blameless.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the six stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's instruction names something only that stage has.

Allowlisting connected apps answers stage 02, so an unapproved Data Loader build cannot be linked at all. The page calls it the control that ends the chain outright.

The API Enabled defense names stage 04's API Enabled permission and its bulk extraction, and it also limits Data Loader use, the tool stage 02 impersonated. Export-volume alerts name stage 04's Transaction Security Policies.

The network defense blocks or challenges authorization attempts, stage 02. Its reason cites the Mullvad and TOR addresses from stage 01, but its instructions name logins and authorizations, not calls, so column 01 is not marked for it.

The human-process defense names stage 01's unsolicited call and stage 02's connection code. The identity defense names stage 05's identity provider, and stage 03's kill switch: find and revoke the grants.

Column 06 is empty. No instruction names the extortion, though the human-process defense says to make reporting blameless because the extortion may arrive months later. That gap is the second discussion question.

== Restrict who can authorize apps, and who can export
layout: compare
col: Allowlist connected apps (02) | Restrict Customize Application and Manage Connected Apps to a few trusted admins | Require approval before any new connected app is authorized | Allowlist known-good apps, so an unapproved Data Loader build cannot link
col: API Enabled as export (02, 04) | Bulk extraction depended on the compromised user's own rights | Restrict API access, and Data Loader use, to people whose jobs require it | Audit profiles and permission sets for accounts that kept it
A user who cannot grant the consent cannot be talked into granting it.
notes:
Two defenses that decide who can grant consent and what a grant can take.

First, allowlist connected apps, and restrict who can authorize one. The page calls this the control that ends the chain outright. Restrict the Customize Application and Manage Connected Apps permissions to a small set of trusted administrators, require an approval process before any new connected app is authorized, and allowlist known-good applications so an unapproved Data Loader build cannot be linked at all. A user who cannot grant the consent cannot be talked into granting it.

Second, treat the API Enabled permission as the export permission it is. Bulk extraction depended on the compromised user's own rights. Restrict API access to people whose jobs require it, limit Data Loader use to that same set, and audit profiles and permission sets regularly for accounts that accumulated the capability and never gave it back. The page says most organizations grant this far more widely than the work requires.

== Alert on export volume; bind access to location
layout: compare
col: Export volume alerts (04) | Shield Transaction Security Policies can fire on large data downloads | Event Monitoring gives per-user API and data-access patterns | Feed both into your SIEM and baseline them
col: Network location (02) | Calls and collection came from Mullvad and TOR addresses | Login and trusted IP ranges on user profiles | IP restrictions in connected app policies
The page says network limits will not stop a determined actor with better infrastructure, but remove the cheap version entirely.
notes:
Two defenses that watch the export and narrow where an authorization can come from.

First, alert on export volume, because the credential and the app will both look legitimate. Salesforce Shield Transaction Security Policies can fire on large data downloads, and Event Monitoring gives per-user API and data-access patterns. Feed both into your SIEM and baseline them. A SIEM is a security information and event management system, which collects logs and alerts on them. The page says the victim who lost only ten percent lost only ten percent because someone noticed the volume.

Second, bind access to network location so VPN-originated authorizations fail. Calls and collection came from Mullvad and TOR addresses. Login IP ranges and trusted IP ranges on user profiles, plus IP restrictions in connected app policies, block or challenge authorization attempts from unexpected sources.

Keep the page's caveat with it. It will not stop a determined actor with better infrastructure, but it removes the cheap version entirely.

== Harden the human process; scope to identity
layout: compare
col: The human process (01, 02) | A staff rule: no app authorization or connection code on an unsolicited call | A verified call-back path to use instead | Training on this scenario, and blameless reporting of suspicious calls
col: Identity and grants (03, 05) | A confirmed vishing success means reviewing the identity provider too | Enumerate every connected app and OAuth grant in your tenant | Confirm a named owner and business reason for each; revoke the rest
The extortion may arrive months later, so one report may be the only early warning available.
notes:
Two defenses for the people and for the response.

First, harden the human process the way you would a technical one. Give staff an unambiguous rule that IT will never ask them to authorize an application or enter a connection code during an unsolicited call, and give them a verified call-back path to use instead. A call-back path means hanging up and calling IT back on a number known to be genuine. Train specifically on this scenario rather than on generic phishing awareness, and make reporting a suspicious call frictionless and blameless. The extortion may arrive months later, so that one report may be the only early warning available.

Second, scope the response to the identity estate, and audit grants you never approved. Because credentials from these calls were reused against Okta and Microsoft 365, a confirmed vishing success means reviewing the identity provider too. Then run the audit that finds the quiet version of this attack: enumerate every connected app and OAuth grant in your tenant, confirm each has a named owner and a business reason, and revoke the rest.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Can only trusted administrators authorize connected apps, and only from an allowlist?
check: Who holds API Enabled, and does each person's job require it?
check: Would a large data export alert anyone, and how fast?
check: Do IP restrictions challenge authorizations from unexpected sources?
check: Do staff know IT never asks for a connection code on an unsolicited call?
check: After a confirmed vishing success, would you review the identity provider too?
check: Does every connected app and OAuth grant have a named owner and business reason?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether only a small set of trusted administrators can authorize connected apps, and whether an unapproved Data Loader build could be linked at all. Ask who holds the API Enabled permission, and whether each person's job requires it.

Ask whether a large data export would alert anyone, and how quickly. The page says detection latency directly determined loss.

Ask whether login IP ranges and connected app IP restrictions would block or challenge an authorization from an unexpected source.

Ask whether staff know the rule that IT will never ask them to authorize an application or enter a connection code during an unsolicited call, and whether they have a verified call-back path.

Finally, ask whether a confirmed vishing success would bring the identity provider into scope, and whether every connected app and OAuth grant in your tenant has a named owner and a business reason.

== What on the screen could have warned the employee?
layout: question
The employee saw the genuine Salesforce domain, the genuine consent page and a tool name they recognized, while the caller, posing as IT support, explained why it was needed.

What on that screen could have told them to stop?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one.

Everything the employee saw was real, the page says. The only false element was the identity of the person asking.

Ask each person to name one thing on a consent screen that would separate this app from the real Data Loader, before you show the answer slide. The answer slide gives what the page says about consent screens, and the defense it offers instead.

== The page says the screen offered nothing to tell them apart
layout: answer
The page says the interface offers nothing that distinguishes this app from the real thing. Its answer: a user who cannot grant the consent cannot be talked into granting it.
- Allowlist connected apps, and restrict who can authorize one.
notes:
The page's answer starts from the screen's weakness.

Consent screens are a poor control at the exact moment they matter most, it says, because a user who has been told to expect one and given a plausible reason will approve it. The interface offers them nothing that distinguishes this app from the real thing.

So the page moves the decision. Restrict who can authorize a connected app, require an approval process before any new one is authorized, and allowlist known-good applications so an unapproved Data Loader build cannot be linked at all.

Bring the group's ideas back to this. An idea that depends on the employee spotting a difference on the screen runs against what the page says: the interface offers nothing that distinguishes this app.

== What would you add for stage 06?
layout: question
No defense on the page has an instruction that names stage 06: UNC6240's 72-hour bitcoin demand, under a claimed ShinyHunters name, often months after the theft.

What would you add, and what would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the gap in the defense matrix.

The page's defenses name stages 01 to 05. None of their instructions names the extortion at stage 06: the demand for bitcoin within 72 hours, by call or email to employees, under a ShinyHunters name that Google treats as likely pressure tactics.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would act on.

== What the page offers for stage 06
layout: answer
No defense's instruction names the extortion, though the human-process defense cites its delay. Stage 06 itself draws two lessons:
- A single employee's vishing report deserves a real investigation.
- A crew name in a ransom note is a marketing claim.
notes:
Be clear about where the page stops. No defense's instruction names the extortion, and this deck adds none of its own.

The page does say two things that bear on it. The human-process defense asks for frictionless, blameless reporting of suspicious calls, because the extortion may arrive months later and that one report may be the only early warning available.

Stage 06 itself draws two consequences. An organization can be months past a successful intrusion with no signal at all, so a vishing report from a single employee deserves a real investigation rather than a note. And a familiar crew name in a ransom note is a marketing claim; treating it as attribution will send an investigation in the wrong direction.

If the group proposes other ideas, treat them as the group's own, and test each against the facts the page reports: the 72-hour bitcoin deadline, demands by call or email to employees, the two tuta addresses the page lists, and the data leak site the actors were preparing.

